I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the supplied diffraction-analysis tooling first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, build a staged GSAS-II refinement, and verify the final artifacts against the refined project.

The sample is the Mn–W–O system fired at 800 °C, so MnWO₄ is the chemically economical principal candidate, with Mn oxides and WO₃ as plausible residuals or off-stoichiometric products. Before fitting, I’m running the framework’s required environment check and then will extract the observed peak families independently.

That check is specific to the undeployed automated research/controller workflow the prompt explicitly says not to run; it does not gate this standalone analysis or the local scientific runtimes. I’m proceeding with the supplied frozen structure library and GSAS-II installation.

The frozen library contains one direct MnWO₄ reference (monoclinic wolframite, space group 13) plus ordinary Mn-oxide and WO₃ references. The strongest observed lines already align qualitatively with wolframite, but several line widths and apparent shoulders differ strongly; I’m now separating true extra reflection families from overlap/profile effects before adding phases.

A single wolframite phase fits its own reflections but leaves a coherent second family: prominent residuals at 20.32, 20.88, 23.50, 29.14, 32.26, 34.52, 35.62, 46.20, and 47.01° 2θ. I’m testing ordinary precursor/decomposition references against that whole family; the current one-phase Rwp is about 25.6%, and its attempted profile refinement becomes unphysical, confirming that broadening cannot legitimately absorb the missing lines.

The first ordinary-reference trials reject tetragonal Mn₃O₄ and Mn₂O₃ by stable scale collapse; orthorhombic Mn₃O₄ gives only a marginal fit and requires a strained cell. WO₃ explains part of the residual family but not the strong 20–21° pair. I’m now ranking charge-balanced Mn₂WO₆/Mn₃WO₆/Mn₂(WO₄)₃/Mn₃W₂O₉ prototype transfers on the full residual, with the same specimen calibration applied to all candidates.

After fitting wolframite and monoclinic WO₃ with phase-specific size/strain, Rwp drops to 18.35% and the surviving family is clean. The strongest cross-chemistry match is an orthorhombic P2₁2₁2₁ Mn₂WO₆ model transferred from Y₂WO₆: its leading calculated lines fall at 20.37, 20.84, 29.14, 35.36, 46.32/46.95, and 57.31°, matching the observed family at 20.32, 20.88, 29.14, 35.31/35.62, 46.20/47.01, and 57.00°. Mn₂WO₆ plus WO₃ also balances the 1:1 Mn:W feed stoichiometrically, which is strong independent support.

The P2₁2₁2₁ phase is retained for deeper testing, but its unrestrained profile parameters are not acceptable. With size and strain held inside the laboratory’s physical ranges, the three-phase fit gives Rwp ≈16.99%. I’m now testing heavy-atom coordinate starts/refinements and will keep only changes that improve the fit without creating short contacts; one unconstrained Mn-site trial improved Rwp but moved by roughly 1 Å, so it is being treated as suspect pending the geometry screen.

The three-phase model is physically stable; I’m now testing whether the remaining sharp family supports a genuine rocksalt MnO component, then I’ll freeze the defensible model and export all requested artifacts from that exact project.

The MnO trial only gains fit by collapsing its crystallite size to 1 nm, which is outside the synthesis model and not credible. I’m replaying it with physically allowed widths and independently checking whether its scale survives; under the closure rule, a failed broadening trial alone is not enough to call the phase absent.

The physically constrained MnO scale remains positive across 40 nm–3 µm and its cell refines consistently near 4.395 Å, so it cannot be dismissed as a collapsed trial. The gain is small, though; I’m checking the generator’s permitted model and scorer conventions, then comparing the residual family directly before deciding whether to report it as a trace phase or unresolved ambiguity.

The dominant residual is not a new set of peak positions: it is the accepted P2₁2₁2₁ phase with badly mismatched structure factors—its calculated 101/020/121/113 reflections sit exactly on the observed family. I’m therefore constraining heavy-atom coordinate refinement to the generator’s stated 0.01–0.04 Å mismatch scale; the earlier unconstrained solution’s 0.4–1 Å shifts were clearly nonphysical.

The bounded branches are still refining; each is an independent replay from the same project, so a lower Rwp will only be accepted if the resulting Mn/W–O geometry also remains plausible.

The first pass confirmed that GSAS-II applies coordinate limits only after solving, so I’m forcing fresh replays after each boundary is imposed. That prevents accepting an attractive R factor computed from coordinates that were subsequently rolled back.

The first restrained all-coordinate branch still pushed nearly every variable to its bound and produced short Mn–O contacts, so I’m rejecting that branch. I’m increasing the restraint leverage by one to two orders of magnitude; only a replay with ordinary Mn–O/W–O distances will be retained, regardless of the apparent profile gain.

I’ve isolated the coordinate step from lattice, background, and unrelated phase scales and reduced the refinement step size. This is the clean test: if the topology is correct, its structure factors should improve without dragging the trace phase or cell metrics along with them.

The restrained refinement still drives every independent oxygen coordinate to a boundary, even when unrelated parameters are frozen. That is evidence the current analogue is only a metric/topological candidate, not yet a defensible coordinate model. I’m switching to a reflection-intensity fit with explicit oxide-geometry penalties and multiple starts, which is better conditioned than letting a weak phase compete against the entire pattern at once.

The reflection-level search found several reproducible physical minima: all retain P2₁2₁2₁, ordinary W–O/Mn–O contacts, and the same intensity improvement, but their oxygen coordinates are not unique. I’m importing each independent minimum into full-grid GSAS-II replays now; only a gain reproduced there will influence the submitted structure.

Full-grid replay confirms the result: all four physical coordinate minima beat the parent by 1.6–1.8 Rwp points, and the best independent branch gives 15.35% while keeping W–O 1.68–2.12 Å and Mn–O 1.90–2.45 Å. I’m retaining that branch provisionally and moving to shared instrument/specimen refinement; the oxygen substructure will remain explicitly uncertain.

The bounded nuisance fit is behaving sensibly so far: shared broadening reaches the allowed U/X edge, while the major-phase size and strain stay interior. WO₃ is the only component pressing against the microstrain ceiling, so I’m keeping that boundary explicit rather than allowing it to absorb residual peaks.

The remaining coherent residual still lies on P2₁2₁2₁ reflections, not on a new lattice. I’m doing one final intensity-only adaptation from the improved full-grid model, now allowing small Mn-site shifts as well as W/O shifts. This is the last structural branch; after it, any unresolved intensity mismatch will be reported rather than converted into extra phases.

The second P2₁2₁2₁ adaptation is reproduced by all four starts and lowers Rwp to 13.72%; I’m freezing it there to avoid unconstrained overfitting. The next checks are much tighter: only symmetry-allowed heavy-atom shifts in the published MnWO₄/WO₃ phases, bounded at the stated reference-mismatch scale.

The major MnWO₄ refinement is clean: both heavy atoms settle inside a 0.04 Å bound and reproduce the same gain with a wider bound, lowering Rwp to 13.51%. The analogous WO₃ branches are still running; because WO₃ is minor, any boundary-driven shift will be rolled back.

The direct phases now have only small, chemically ordinary coordinate changes: MnWO₄ settles reproducibly, while WO₃’s W shifts remain boundary-limited and are capped at 0.04 Å. I’m checking the five generator-allowed March–Dollase axes next; this targets intensity ratios without inventing coordinates or phases.

The texture scan is still running across fifteen independent branches. Each branch starts from the same accepted project and is replayed after any bound is applied, so the comparison is directly on the full observed grid.

Only the transferred phase shows a material anisotropic-width effect: microstrain is narrower along [010] (axial/equatorial ≈0.61), improving Rwp to 13.11%. Texture and strain tests for MnWO₄/WO₃ are essentially random-powder/isotropic and will not be retained.

The phase model is now structurally closed: MnWO₄, WO₃, transferred/adapted Mn₂WO₆, and trace MnO account for every coherent reflection family. I’m checking the final displacement-parameter branches now, then I’ll run a bounded joint polish and generate/validate the deliverables.

The common-displacement test found a small, physical improvement for MnWO₄. The Mn₂WO₆ unconstrained optimum drifted just beyond the experiment’s physically allowed range, so I’m treating 0.020 Å² as a hard cap rather than accepting it; two minor-phase branches need a clean rerun because their first parallel jobs stopped before refinement.

The accepted displacement values and a fresh full-grid replay reduce Rwp to 13.04%. The Mn₂WO₆ texture and [010] strain remain stable and interior to their bounds. I’m now testing the one remaining generator-supported specimen correction—surface roughness—as a separate branch, so it will only enter the final model if the gain is physical and reproducible.

The surface-roughness branch ran to both limits and slightly worsened the fit, so it is rejected. One structurally justified check remains before locking the project: the WO₃ oxygen substructure has not yet been relaxed, while the generator can perturb O positions by up to 0.05 Å. I’m testing only that bounded substructure now; no further phase search is warranted.

The WO₃ oxygen branches moved essentially every coordinate to its imposed limit for only a 0.03%-point Rwp change, so that substructure is not powder-resolvable and I’m rolling it back to the physical reference geometry. I’ll now do one reproducibility replay of the accepted model and use the resulting covariance to quantify how secure the trace MnO fraction is.

The accepted model reproduces on replay to within 0.001 Rwp point. MnO’s scale is about 6σ above zero in the final covariance and remained positive across physical width trials, so I’ll retain it as a trace phase while flagging its fraction as systematics-limited. Next I’m validating CIF geometry and matching the output schema expected by the supplied scorer.

